think checked which rooms already carried an allow row before adding anything: risala/ and open/ were both allowed in an earlier round this same session, and fleet/ (opened this round) had none -- grepped for it directly rather than assuming a room this new might already be covered
think grepped fleet/'s own three files for "Keaton" before choosing a classification, per the same discipline the risala/open rounds used -- zero hits, so fleet/ ships template rather than scrub, the clean-room classification rather than the name-transform one
think did not add expanding-prompts/ to the allowlist even though this round wrote a new file there, since the operator's own request named "these new folders" -- open/ and fleet/ -- and the whole expanding-prompts/ directory carries no allow row by deliberate, standing policy (its own manifest comment calls it one of several "personal-heavy dirs" staying withheld until each file earns its place one at a time); opening the whole room was not asked for and would have been a scope decision this round had no standing to make on its own
think ran the projection and the full witness before trusting the manifest edit, per the same TAME lens every prior seed round in this session used -- SOW_WITNESS_GREEN, M1_OK, and grepped the projected seed/fleet/ and seed/open/ directories directly for "Keaton," confirming zero occurrences across all twelve files between the two rooms
think ran the bare publish-seed.sh first, fetched the current public HEAD, and diffed against it before pushing -- eighteen files changed, and every one of them was something this round or the round just before it actually touched: README, MAP, fleet/, open/, the two scrub-hardening files, and the four witness-repair files, nothing unexpected in the diff
obs this was the smallest, cleanest custody-gate crossing of the day -- one file added to the manifest, one projection, one witness run, one diff review, one push -- because every room being published had already been proven clean in the rounds that built it, rather than needing first-time scrutiny at publish time
